#|
EXERCISE 4
Use recursion to write a function that accepts a string and returns the
first index that contains the character “x”. For example, the string,
"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz" has an “x” at index 23. To keep things simple,
assume the string definitely has at least one “x”.

answer:|#

; lets assume input is list of symbols for simplicity
; list-of-symbol -> number
(define (contains-x? los)
  ; regular recursio nversion
  (cond [(null? los) #f] ;safe guard
        [(equal? (car los) 'x) 0]
        [else (+ 1 (contains-x? (cdr los)))]
        )

  ; accumulator version
  #|
  (define (helper xs i)
    (cond [(null? xs) #f] ;safe guard
          [(equal? (car xs) 'x) i]
          [else (+ 1 (contains-x? (cdr xs)))]
          )
    )
  (helper los 0)
  |#
  
  
  )
(contains-x? '(a b c d e f g h i j k l m n o p q r s t u v w x y z))

