#|
EXERCISE 5
This problem is known as the unique paths problem: Let’s say you have a
grid of rows and columns. Write a function that accepts a number of rows
and a number of columns and calculates the number of possible “shortest”
paths from the upper-leftmost square to the lower-rightmost square.
For example, here’s what the grid looks like with three rows and seven
columns. You want to get from the S (Start) to the F (Finish).

By “shortest” path, I mean that at every step, either you’re moving one
step to the right:

Or you’re moving one step downward:
Again, your function should calculate the number of shortest paths

answer:
|#

; number number -> number
(define (unique-paths r c)
  ; calc shortest path to the left
  ; calc shortest path down
  ; stop when col reaches c and stop when bottom reaches r

  ; note that row and col is 1 based
  (define (unique-paths-helper curr-r curr-c)
    (cond [(and (>= curr-r r) (>= curr-c c)) 1]
          [(or (> curr-r r) (> curr-c c)) 0]
          [else (+ (unique-paths-helper (+ curr-r 1) curr-c)
                   (unique-paths-helper curr-r (+ curr-c 1))
                   )])
    )
  (unique-paths-helper 1 1)
  
  )
(unique-paths 2 2)
(unique-paths 3 1)